WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:08.480
In this series of videos, we're going to discuss some of the environments of ZOS UNIX,

2
00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.440
such as the language environment, debugging, and callable services.

3
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:18.160
We're going to split the discussion between application programming in ZOS UNIX,

4
00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:24.720
so we're talking here about things like compiling, running, porting, and debugging applications,

5
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:30.800
and some additional considerations like access control lists and data sharing.

6
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.200
So let's get started.

7
00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:39.680
Shown here is an example of a C program which reads data from a hierarchical file

8
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:43.120
and displays the results on the screen.

9
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:53.840
The ZOS UNIX C89 CC C++ utility is used to compile and link edit ZOS UNIX C and ZOS applications.

10
00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.800
Programs can be compiled and linked edited in one step,

11
00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:02.400
or the link edit of the object modules can be performed in a separate step from the compilation.

12
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.920
In order for the C89 CC and C++ to perform C and C++ compiles,

13
00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:14.560
the ZOS C and C++ optional feature must be installed on the system.

14
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:20.560
ZOS UNIX supports different environments where ZOS UNIX C and MVS applications can run.

15
00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:25.280
A ZOS UNIX shell program can be run by specifying the program name on the command line,

16
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.400
or the program can be called from a shell script.

17
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:32.000
Also, a program residing in a ZOS dataset can be run from the shell

18
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.920
by using a tsoe call command to pass a call to the program through tsoe.

19
00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:44.480
Applications can also run in a ZOS UNIX shell in the ZOS batch by using the bpx bags program,

20
00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:50.880
or a ZOS UNIX program which resides in a ZOS dataset can be run from tsoe.

21
00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.680
A ZOS UNIX program which resides in a ZOS dataset can be run in batch

22
00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.800
using the JCL exec statement to start the program.

23
00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:04.000
The appropriate locale for an application can be set by using the set locale function.

24
00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:10.400
For example, an application can define set locale LC underscore all comma C,

25
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:15.520
meaning all the categories of variables must comply to the default C language definition,

26
00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:21.120
or another example is set locale LC underscore monetary comma UK,

27
00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.040
which would use the UK English monetary definition of pound sterling.

28
00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:31.440
Enhanced ASCII support with file tagging and auto conversion makes it easier to port

29
00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.280
UNIX programs from the original ASCII environment to the ZOS environment,

30
00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:40.320
which is an EBSIDIC environment. Before this support, if the original program

31
00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:43.520
was written with ASCII dependent data handling in C or C++,

32
00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:47.760
it might not be ported directly to ZOS without changes in the program.

33
00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:52.640
The CCSID of a program is set at compile time.

34
00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:58.000
The CCSID tag of file can be set by the ch tag shell command.

35
00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:01.840
With auto conversion, an EBSIDIC program does not need to know

36
00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:05.040
that it is reading and writing to an ASCII file.

37
00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.120
Conversion is done if it needs to be.

38
00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:14.320
ZOS system components need fast any mode code page conversion and other services.

39
00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:19.360
The solution is Unicode conversion services, along with IBM Z hardware assists.

40
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.960
ZOS can provide support for the conversion of Unicode data,

41
00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:29.760
either UTF-8 or UTF-16, to any of the EBSIDIC CCSIDs currently supported by your applications.

42
00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.280
If you intend to make use of this capability, you must enable the ZOS conversion services

43
00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:41.040
and install a conversion image which specifies the conversion that you want your application to perform.

44
00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:46.640
IBM Ported Tools for ZOS is a non-priced program product

45
00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:50.880
designed to deliver tools and applications for the ZOS platform.

46
00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:56.400
These applications have been modified to operate within the ZOS environment.

47
00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:03.120
Ported Tools for ZOS includes the Perl scripting language as well as the OpenSSH secure shell.

48
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.480
After your C or C++ program has been developed,

49
00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.080
you can take advantage of the ZOS Unix Debugger with its DBX utility

50
00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:15.840
to debug the program from within the shell environment on a ZOS system.

51
00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.760
DBX is a process-oriented debugger. It runs in a different process or address space than

52
00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:27.040
the process being debugged. It provides multi-process debug across fork exec and

53
00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:32.160
has the capability to attach to and debug a process that is already executing.

54
00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.680
DBX debugged signals that are sent to the process being debugged.

55
00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:41.440
It also debugs at machine level using machine registers and examines memory addresses.

56
00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:48.080
High level languages or HLLs which support calls to assembler subroutines

57
00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:50.880
can use the ZOS Unix callable services.

58
00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:56.800
The application programmer is responsible for ensuring that the correct environment exists.

59
00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:01.840
One reason to use the callable services from HLLs is to get access to data in the

60
00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:08.240
hierarchical file system. The concept of sockets provides the application programmer

61
00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:13.200
with an interface which is very similar to that used for file IO in the C language.

62
00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:18.400
The IO operations used for file access and network access have the same names but the

63
00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:24.480
functions performed are different. User-written socket applications can use TCPIP services as a

64
00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:30.720
communication vehicle. Both client and server socket applications can use the socket interface

65
00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:36.160
to communicate over the internet and between other socket applications by using local sockets.

66
00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:42.400
An assembler interface is also provided for those applications that do not use the C or C++

67
00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:50.400
runtime library. ZOS Unix supports two types of socket families. Unix domain sockets,

68
00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:55.760
also called local sockets, are part of the Unix address family AF underscore Unix.

69
00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:02.400
AF underscore Unix sockets provide communication between processes on a single system and internet

70
00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:08.480
protocol sockets are part of the internet address family AF underscore INET for IPv4 and AF underscore

71
00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:15.120
INET6 for IPv6. In the next video we'll look at printing and ACLs.
